z4i = z1z + ledger (bots/zl0) + THROW_SAFE (new switch; Cfg.THROW_SAFE; Baby.gridKings, tryThrow). tryThrow puts our
kings from the king channel (Comms.kings, their 3x3 bodies) on the flight grid, so a flight through an unseen king of
ours scores as an own hit (-damage, as for a seen one). And it does not throw while the best flight scores below zero:
the carry goes on (it ends by MAX_CARRY_DURATION 10) instead of THROW_HOLD's round-6 release of any flight. Both hooks
are ledgered (G.settle, K 18 and 20). THROW_CHECK (u1k, neutral on u0m) did more (a king hit scored 3x, holds until
THROW_LATE, a re-check after the turn) and is not ledgered.

Evidence (replica dumps, 89 cached, deduplicated by game; scratch ownhit.py, kinghits.py): our thrown rats hit our own
king 49 times; 22 of the 29 with a known carry length were thrown on carry round 6, and the king's centre was outside
the thrower's cone in 22 of 29. Over the cache, our throws dealt our own king 3,648 damage (theirs dealt theirs 888),
140-194 a game on thunderdome vs Varun, a cell lost in both seats. Locally it is rare (z1z's 20 games in
~/diag26b/sl: 2 hits, 48 damage), so the mechanism is read on the replica.

Falsifiers, registered before any z4i game:
1. Screen zd181 (vs zd136) on the s6 gates: t <= -1.5, picks/100 per-cell z <= -2, ppr per-round z <= -2, transfer
   per round < 0.97x, any overrun.
2. Replica: the block rules plus the transfer floor against the twin. Mechanism: our throws' hits on our own king in
   the candidate's block games (kinghits.py) must be at most a third of the twin's in the same blocks. Any cell it
   flips must hold a twin game with an own-king hit or a carry held past round 6; a flip elsewhere comes from the
   ledger's drift, and two such flips the wrong way drop it.
